Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Three Strings Through Station Six
- Story & Context: At 102 BPM, Aizada begins with one two-chord komuz vamp in CLAMP state: palm-muted thumb strokes with nearly no open resonance. Kabelo answers every second Palm Gate with a round kick and short analog-bass fifth. After sixteen bars the harmony remains unchanged, but Aizada moves into RAIL, letting repeated down-up strokes cross the upper two strings while deliberately omitting the lowest string. Meerim adds clipped clavinet sus2 dyads in the newly opened low-mid gap. Thabiso replaces one programmed clap with a frame-drum rim hit every fourth cycle. The first major lift comes not from a chord change but from a full RELEASE across all three strings, at which point hats disappear and Elias enters with one warm bass-flugelhorn pedal. The track closes by returning to the original CLAMP pattern, making the same vamp feel physically smaller without reducing volume.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Old cableway route diagram marked STATION 6 with three parallel copper lines running through a switchback, one line temporarily missing in the middle section, a komuz hand diagram beside a mechanical brake lever. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “station six,” “hold three,” “leave one out,” and “open now.”

Track 2: Palm Gate Before the Downhill Car
- Story & Context: At 108 BPM, Kabelo opens with the EP’s heaviest kwaito kick pattern while Aizada uses RAIL on the outer two komuz strings. Every fourth bar ends with a hard Palm Gate, producing a completely dry stop that cues either bass, frame drum, or silence. The arrangement turns the location after each gate into a rotating response: first analog bass, then dabyl-style center hit, then clavinet octave, then nothing. Midway through, Aizada shifts to FAN without changing chord, forcing Kabelo to strip away hats and upper percussion so the wide wrist-driven strum can dominate the spectrum. Elias answers selected FAN releases with short muted falling thirds. The final section alternates one bar of FAN with one bar of CLAMP, creating the impression of the whole track expanding and contracting while the harmonic vamp remains fixed.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Cable-car brake schematic with one heavy black handprint stamped across the line just before a downhill arrow, four response boxes labeled BASS / SKIN / KEY / EMPTY, steel cable strands in shallow focus. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “gate first,” “car downhill,” “close the hand,” and “nothing after.”

Track 3: Switchback Eleven Leaves the Lowest String Out
- Story & Context: The closer runs at 98 BPM and begins almost drumless. Aizada repeats a slow open-fifth komuz figure using only the upper two strings, intentionally excluding the lowest string for the first two minutes. Meerim plays isolated prepared-clavinet fifths while Thabiso uses only fingertip frame-drum taps. Kabelo introduces a short bass root after every second phrase, carefully leaving the missing acoustic low string perceptible as an absence. At the midpoint, Aizada adds the lowest string for the first time but remains in CLAMP, so the new pitch is present without full resonance. Later she moves through RAIL and finally RELEASE, allowing all three strings to ring while the drums become simpler rather than larger. Elias enters on a sustained low fifth during the final RELEASE. The last gesture is a single all-string strum followed by an immediate Palm Gate, ending the EP on hand-made silence rather than decay.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Mountain service-track engineering map labeled SWITCHBACK 11 with two bright parallel lines and one faint missing lower line that reappears near the bottom of the page, a hand resting over three komuz strings, oxidized cable pulley beside it. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “eleven turns,” “lowest missing,” “bring it back,” and “close at once.”

